# Caddy in front of Yuva, with certificates for the own domains of status pages, help centers,
# roadmap boards and changelogs.
#
# YUVA_PUBLIC_URL=https://support.example.com, YUVA_STATUS_DOMAINS=ask (the default) and
# YUVA_STATUS_CNAME=support.example.com (also the default). A customer points
# status.customer.example at support.example.com with a CNAME and adds the TXT record
# _yuva-status.status.customer.example the panel shows. Once Yuva has seen both records the
# domain is verified, and the first visit makes Caddy ask
#   GET http://127.0.0.1:8080/status-domains/check?domain=status.customer.example
# on the same Yuva server. It answers 200 only for the verified domain of a published
# page in a live workspace and 404 for anything else, so Caddy issues certificates only
# for those, and stops renewing one when its domain is removed or turned off. The
# endpoint is public (rate-limited per address) and reveals only domains that already
# serve public pages, so it needs no secret.
#
# Port 80 must reach Caddy for the HTTP challenge, and 443 for TLS-ALPN.

{
	on_demand_tls {
		ask http://127.0.0.1:8080/status-domains/check
	}
}

support.example.com {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8080 {
		header_up X-Real-IP {remote_host}
	}
}

# Every other name: own domains of status pages, help centers, roadmap boards and changelogs.
# Yuva serves only that page there.
https:// {
	tls {
		on_demand
	}
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8080 {
		header_up X-Real-IP {remote_host}
	}
}
